<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Punic Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/264-bce">264</a>–<a href="/years/146-bce">146 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Punic Wars were three wars fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> between 264 and 146 BCE. They included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>, and ended with the destruction of Carthage. The conflicts ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a> and transformed Rome into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> imperial power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/punic-wars.jpg" alt="Punic Wars" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carnby · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Domain_changes_during_the_Punic_Wars_BC.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Punic Wars</b> fundamentally shaped Rome’s imperial trajectory, compelling it to build a permanent navy and to develop provincial government, beginning with Sicily, transforming it from an Italian power into a Mediterranean empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Punic Wars</b> (264–146 BCE) generals built many temples to fulfil their vows; the Magna Mater was brought from Pessinus in 206 BCE and installed on the Palatine in 191, and a mystery cult of Ceres and Proserpina came in 205 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Elea allied with Rome in the early 3rd century BCE and supplied ships in the <b>Punic Wars</b>; Rome recruited priestesses of Ceres from Velia.</blockquote><a href="/state/elea">Elea</a></li></ul></section>
